\DOC WINDOWS_X86_WRAP_NOSTACK_TAC

\TYPE {WINDOWS_X86_WRAP_NOSTACK_TAC : thm -> thm -> thm -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Proves a Windows-ABI {ensures} specification for a wrapped x86 routine from the
corresponding System V specification, either closing the goal or reducing it to
a residual event-trace obligation.

\DESCRIBE
The arguments are {winmc : thm}, the definition (or name) of the
Windows-wrapper machine-code list occurring in the goal; {stdmc : thm}, the
definition of the standard (non-Windows) machine-code list; and {coreth : thm},
the already-proved specification of the core routine stated in the System V
calling convention, which supplies the functional/safety content being
transported. It applies to a goal {A ?- t} in which {t} is a universally
quantified Windows-ABI {ensures} statement, possibly prefixed by an existential
quantifier over an event-trace function. Either the goal is closed with no
subgoals, or exactly one subgoal remains, leaving {A} otherwise untouched:
{
   A ?- ?e. !x1 ... xn. hyps ==> ensures ...
   =================================================  WINDOWS_X86_WRAP_NOSTACK_TAC winmc stdmc coreth
   A, hyps', z1 ... zp ?- residual existential statement about the concatenated event list
}
\noindent Here the outer existential witness has been instantiated and the
quantifiers {x1 ... xn} and the implication stripped; {hyps'} are all the
hypotheses of the original implication in a normalised form, with
non-overlapping conditions expanded into their modular form; and {z1 ... zp}
are many new assumptions describing the final machine state: register and
memory reads at the exit state, the {bytes_loaded} hypothesis, a {MAYCHANGE}
relation between the initial and final states, the event-list value, and the
properties of the core routine's event trace coming from {coreth}.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only these two outcomes — closing the goal, or
producing this single event-trace subgoal — and only for goals of this
wrapper-specification shape with no prior assumptions.

\ENDDOC
